---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../lib/url';
import { templates } from '../lib/templates';
import { sampleProject, sampleFilename } from '../lib/samples';
import { CardView } from '../components/shared';
const t = templates.find((t) => t.id === 'sight-word-bingo')!;
const p = sampleProject(t);
---

<Layout
  title="Classroom Bingo: Choose, Print and Host an Activity"
  description="A practical teacher guide to letter, number and vocabulary Bingo: choose grids, print different cards for a class, save the project, and check the same activity."
>
  <section class="page-intro">
    <div class="eyebrow">FOR TEACHERS</div><h1>
      A classroom game,<br /><em>ready for your group.</em>
    </h1><p>
      Start with the skill you want learners to practice, then prepare one
      consistent set of cards and calls.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="template-detail">
    <article class="article">
      <h2>Choose the learning task</h2><p>
        For letter identification, use <a
          href={withBase('/templates/alphabet-bingo/')}>Alphabet Bingo</a
        > and choose uppercase or lowercase for that round. Call the letter name;
        the two cases are independent presets.
      </p><p>
        For numeral recognition, <a
          href={withBase('/templates/numbers-1-20-bingo/')}>Numbers 1–20</a
        > pairs spoken names with printed numerals on a 3 × 3 grid. It is not an
        arithmetic or numbered-ball game.
      </p><p>
        For familiar word review, try <a
          href={withBase('/templates/sight-word-bingo/')}>Sight Word Bingo</a
        >, <a href={withBase('/templates/classroom-objects-bingo/')}
          >Classroom Objects</a
        > or <a href={withBase('/templates/food-vocabulary-bingo/')}
          >Food Vocabulary</a
        >. Teach or review unfamiliar words before playing. Read the displayed
        word first; add a short sentence or real-object cue afterward.
      </p>
    </article><div class="template-sample">
      <CardView project={p} card={p.cards[0]} /><p>
        Fixed Sight Word sample: 3 × 3, FREE center.
      </p><a
        href={withBase(`/samples/${sampleFilename(t.id)}`)}
        data-sample-download={t.id}
        download>Download example game pack ↗</a
      ><p>
        This example is separate from your browser activity. Generate your own
        set before web hosting.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <article class="article">
    <section>
      <h2>Choose a grid that fits the lesson</h2><p>
        3 × 3 has eight called entries with FREE or nine without it. It gives
        learners fewer spaces to scan and usually makes a shorter round. 5 × 5
        has 24 or 25 entries, allowing a broader review and a longer search. It
        needs that many distinct entries in your actual edited list. The
        20-number preset cannot fill 5 × 5; duplicate entries do not solve a
        shortage.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Prepare different cards for the whole class</h2><ol>
        <li>
          Open a template, review the list, and set Number of cards to your
          learner count, from 1 to 200. The template loads as edits; it does not
          replace the saved activity immediately.
        </li><li>
          Choose the grid and FREE setting. Generate once and confirm the
          replacement only when ready. Each generated card has a distinct layout
          and a full card ID; simultaneous winners are still possible.
        </li><li>
          Download the game pack and save its project JSON. For 30 learners at
          two per sheet, the PDF has 15 player sheets plus caller and rules
          pages. Keep the ID on every cut card readable.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Match paper and space</h2><p>
        Use A4 or US Letter to match the paper in your printer. One card per
        page gives the most room for large print and handwriting; two is a
        useful classroom balance; four saves paper, especially for 3 × 3. Long
        phrases in 5 × 5 may need fewer per page. Print a test sheet at Actual
        size / 100% and check it from a learner’s seat. See the <a
          href={withBase('/printing-guide/')}>printing guide</a
        >.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Host and check the same activity</h2><p>
        Choose Start hosting in the maker after generating. Call the displayed
        entry and move through the saved calling order. Undo reverses one call;
        advancing restores it. When a learner claims a row, column or diagonal,
        enter the full printed card ID to check it against the calls. FREE
        starts marked. The checker verifies a line, not who finished first.
      </p><p>
        Save JSON after calling if you need to continue on another device.
        Import that JSON there, confirming replacement of its current activity.
        The file contains the actual cards and calling progress; the PDF alone
        cannot restore the project. Refresh normally restores the latest local
        activity when storage is available.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>After printing, keep the card set</h2><p>
        Do not generate again just to open the host or change paper. Generation
        makes a new project with new IDs and resets calls; edited drafts are not
        the printed cards. Save the old JSON before any replacement. If edits
        are pending at download time, choose Download current card set to retain
        the existing cards. Choose Generate new set &amp; download only when you
        intend to print a new activity.
      </p><p>
        For another round with the same cards, use Start a new round in the
        host. It clears calls and reshuffles the order while retaining the card
        set. For a screen-free activity, use the printed caller list and keep a
        paper call record; paper calls do not update the web checker.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Other ways to prepare</h2><p>
        <a href={withBase('/blank-bingo-cards/')}>Blank handwriting grids</a> let
        learners fill cards by hand, with manual calling and checking only. For seasonal
        word review, explore <a
          href={withBase('/templates/thanksgiving-bingo/')}
          >Thanksgiving Word Bingo</a
        > or <a href={withBase('/templates/christmas-bingo/')}
          >Christmas Words</a
        >. <a href={withBase('/templates/')}
          >Browse all {templates.length} templates</a
        >.
      </p><a
        class="button primary"
        href={withBase('/?template=sight-word-bingo')}
        data-template-use="sight-word-bingo"
        data-template-size="3">Prepare a Sight Word activity ↗</a>
    </section>
  </article></Layout
>
